findings.body:
If `origin` is `https://evil.example`, this returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.example` plus `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. Any site can then `fetch` this API with `credentials: "include"` and the browser will expose authenticated responses (session cookies).

Native HTTP clients ignore CORS, so they do not need these headers. Webviews send an Origin and need an explicit allowlist of known app origins. `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with credentials is also invalid per the Fetch spec, so the null-origin fallback is rejected by browsers.

Restore the allowlist and echo Origin (with credentials) only for matching origins; expand the set if webviews use additional known schemes.

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return {};
```
